      "short-container-title": [],
      "abstract": "<jats:p>We analyze a hiring interview in which the firm chooses the quality of an AI\n&lt;br&gt;\nthat drafts each answer, and candidates decide how carefully to verify it. Because a\n&lt;br&gt;\ncorrect draft passes whoever reviews it, AI quality determines how often candidates\n&lt;br&gt;\nmust catch errors, and hence how visible supervision skill is. Informativeness about\n&lt;br&gt;\nsupervision is zero without AI and, once AI is present, strictly declines as AI quality\n&lt;br&gt;\nrises; informativeness about AI-operation skill peaks at an intermediate quality. In\n&lt;br&gt;\na minimal hiring problem, weaker interview AI yields strictly more productive hires\n&lt;br&gt;\nwhenever the role weights supervision enough.</jats:p>",
